d  what is the reason that people are  actually getting abortions i'm more  upset with the supreme court because i  feel like their decision that they've  made is actually not reflective of what  americans  largely polled um have indicated that  they believe do you any of you  you know have  girlfriends or friends or anyone that  you said like here's where we can find  common ground is it just either or  so actually i changed my stance on the  position when i was growing up so i came  from a very low income background i was  only told this narrative that  abortion is the way to go and anyone  who's against abortion doesn't like  women they don't support women  and that quite frankly isn't true you  reject the pro-life label  because it has come to mean so many  things and i think  again like  the super mainstream  movement is  super hypocritical i think there's  there's no other way to say it my  viewpoints don't come from religion  don't come from anything else it comes  from this fact that each human being is  distinct and  um  important and should be respected and  valued and so i'm against abortion in  the same way i'm against police  brutality and torturous acts within  prisons and  the death penalty it seems like one  thing you agree with  whether you're on  the left or the right or somewhere in  between  is that  a lot more should be done for women  and  children you're saying that the  anti-abortion movement could be doing  more  yeah and they should be and they should  have been the whole time and i am so  annoyed every time i hear that now the  real work begins because it's no  you should have been doing it what about  the  abortion rights movement the bigger  problem is that we can have all these  conversations but when you have white  christian male viewpoints making the  decisions at the end of the day or the  ones that are the loudest on tv in  social media then that is the rhetoric  that gets passed and we miss the points  of listening to all the other issues or  figuring out all the things that  surround this decision to support  maternal health or  prenatal postnatal care you're  anti-abortion do you think that the  movement should focus its efforts now on  changing the laws in states like  maryland or  the district of columbia or california  to make it more restrictive let's look  at all these  underlying issues that are the root  causes of why people are having to make  these decisions the danger in in pushing  for abortion is is just like you like  you don't think you have another option  like you're just told this is what you  do isn't that the opposite then  like if when you're saying if you feel  like the only option is abortion  but if you take that option away the  only option is life you're not giving a  choice  it will be decided on the state level uh  in that sense but i would say  that  but on an individual level  it takes that away from the mom or the  pregnant person well i would say  actually that if we're not focusing on  the legal issue of abortion then perhaps  we would be focusing our attention on  all these other supports i don't think  any one of us here is satisfied with  how politicians  don't do what we ask them to do they're  not listening to the problems so does  the idea that it is now going to be  politicians that get to decide  this issue  of abortion  scare you  um  honestly it doesn't scare me i try to  get involved in things that  reflect the way that i would like the  world to be  and i don't think that we consider  enough  how  what we do on our daily lives can affect  even the five of us here talking about  abortion today is that a positive thing  it is  talking about this because i was a  little concerned about coming here  and what i found is that there is a lot  of commonality in what we have to say  and i hope that  the viewers actually listen to it and  think about that and stop thinking about  okay  do you have access yes or no but what  about the other things those other  things are so much more important i'm  very impressed by each of these women  that i'm joined here today with i don't  like that like we've been pitted against  each other when i feel like we would be  more powerful working together i  understand that it's nuanced i  understand it's complex i feel like the  pro-life movement is not doing enough  and i feel like the pro-choice movement  is not doing enough  to  support women and i feel like that's  that's what's really important and  that's not getting heard i don't think  any of us think feel that  abortions are everyone's number one  choice right i think we would like to  create a world where that's not what  women want and women need and so looking  at it that way i think there's  there's common ground that we can  come together on to try and create more  of that kind of a world  now the women that we talked to are from  maryland and the washington dc area and  we had this interview planned for friday  morning before we knew that the court  was going to drop its decision over  turning roe versus wade so when some of  the women arrived at the hotel where we  spoke with them they had no idea that  roe was history  back to you guys that was a very  intelligent coherent conversation so we  do know it can be done but jane i like  the point you raise is it either or it's  so deeply political so deeply polarizing  where do we go from here  right and is consensus even possible  that is the question that is possible  and what are your thoughts jan you've  been covering this from the very  beginning and you've seen it up close  and personal you've talked to many  people on both sides of the issue almost  30 years of work yeah i mean it's  amazing  working we've come to this moment  well i mean i think that in many ways we  saw this on friday we had an idea that  this was going to happen right and yet  everyone was shocked on both sides like  this really did happen but i mean this  is part of an orchestrated campaign tha